    <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-4">{d.scienceSub}</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-xs">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-primary font-mono">01.</span>
          Centrifugal Ligament Shearing
        </h4>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          High-speed disc rotation forces liquid through radial vanes at high acceleration. At the wheel rim, centrifugal forces overcome surface tension and viscous forces, stretching the liquid sheet into Rayleigh-Taylor filaments that disintegrate into micro-droplets governed by Dombrowski-Masters atomization equations: Dv50 &prop; (&mu;^0.1 &middot; &sigma;^0.1) / (&rho;^0.5 &middot; &omega;^0.8 &middot; r^0.6).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-primary font-mono">02.</span>
          Glass Transition Stickiness (Tg)
        </h4>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Food powders with high amorphous carbohydrate content (lactose, fructose, maltodextrin) exhibit a sticky point temperature roughly 10°C to 20°C above their glass transition temperature (T_sticky &approx; Tg + 15&deg;C). If droplet skin temperature exceeds T_sticky when impacting the chamber wall, powder particles adhere permanently, creating insulating build-up cakes.
        </p>
